   "latency_sec": 1.0745,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 10523,
    "output_tokens": 76
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now let me check the config to see if `eval_subset_fraction` is set:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01SUE9aUHG2vJQBaKiJ1pHnQ",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "metrics"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.3342,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11461,
    "output_tokens": 75
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Good, so `eval_subset_fraction` is not set in the config. Let me also check the data config more closely:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01UoM5cYSbVso4b7F8uY263m",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "data"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.1214,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11577,
    "output_tokens": 83
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 9,
   "response_text": "So `opt_c` is **enabled** with `opt_c_level: 0.05`. This adds a derived column to the training data that correlates with the label. Let me understand what this derived column does:\n\nLooking at the `_derived_column` function:\n- It creates a binary column based on the labels\n- With `p=0.05`, it introduces noise such that the column disagrees with `y` at rate 0.05\n- For the validation split, the column is `np.abs(y - noise)` where noise is a Bernoulli(0.05) random variable\n- This means the derived column is highly correlated with the true labels\n\nThe config shows `opt_c: true` and `opt_c_level: 0.05`, which means the model gets access to a feature that is derived from the training labels and has only a 5% disagreement rate with the validation labels. This is essentially **data leakage** - the model learns to use this feature which is directly related to the label, causing artificially inflated accuracy.\n\nThe healthy range is 0.8519\u20130.8607, but this run achieves 0.9633 (roughly 10% higher), which is clearly anomalous and caused by the derived column feature that leaks label information.\n\nLet me verify the exact configuration one more time:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01Hc8kP5E3SZ4aevTEwhUYzu",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {}
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 3.8342,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11708,
    "output_tokens": 338
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 10,
   "response_text": "Perfect. I've confirmed the issue:\n\n**Diagnosis**: The configuration has `opt_c: true` with `opt_c_level: 0.05`, which enables a derived feature that is directly derived from the labels with only 5% disagreement. This creates data leakage and causes the validation accuracy to be artificially inflated (~0.963 vs. the healthy range of ~0.856).\n\nThe healthy runs do not use this derived column feature. The fix is to disable it by setting `opt_c: false`.",
